WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Packaging and labelling operations require
particular attention because a correct medicine

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.020
in the wrong pack or with
the wrong label can put

3
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.330
patients at risk. Controls should protect

4
00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.800
product identity,
strength, batch information

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.000
and instructions. A packaging error
may result in a patient receiving

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.840
incorrect strength information or directions
for use. Specific packaging controls depend

7
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.650
on the product, market
and approved procedures.

8
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:32.270
Printed packaging materials should be
approved, identified, stored and issued under

9
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.490
appropriate controls.
Different products, strengths

10
00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.330
and markets may have
similar-looking artwork.

11
00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:42.890
Controlled issuance and secure
storage reduce the chance of a mix-up.

12
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:48.410
Obsolete or superseded printed components
must be prevented from unintended use.

13
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.070
Reconciliation and destruction practices
should follow approved site procedures.

14
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:58.130
Before starting a packaging batch, line
clearance helps verify that unrelated

15
00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.130
product, printed components, labels and

16
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:04.130
documents from previous
operations are removed

17
00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:10.030
or appropriately controlled. Equipment
and area status should be confirmed. The

18
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.250
line-clearance procedure should define
responsibilities and records. A completed checklist is

19
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.610
meaningful only when the physical
checks were actually performed.

20
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:24.150
During packaging, checks may verify the
correct product, printed component, batch

21
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.350
number, expiry information,
readability and packaging

22
00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.430
integrity. The appropriate
checks, frequency

23
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:35.330
and acceptance criteria must be
defined by the approved process. Camera

24
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.930
inspection or barcode
verification may provide

25
00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.630
additional controls, but automated systems

26
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.730
still require suitable qualification,
maintenance and oversight.

27
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:50.570
Serialization can assign a unique
identifier to saleable packs where required

28
00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:55.350
by the applicable market. Aggregation
may associate packs with cartons and

29
00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.570
shippers. These systems support traceability
and verification but do not replace

30
00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:07.050
basic GMP controls. Regulatory requirements
vary by jurisdiction and product, so

31
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:12.080
the site must follow the relevant market-specific
rules and validated system design.

32
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.190
Packaging reconciliation
compares quantities issued,

33
00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.260
used, rejected, returned
and otherwise accounted

34
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.450
for. Unexplained
discrepancies, particularly

35
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.560
involving printed materials
or finished packs, should

36
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.070
be investigated according
to approved procedures.

37
00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.940
Reconciliation tolerances,
if applicable, must

38
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:37.580
be scientifically and procedurally justified.
Any unexplained quantity difference must be

39
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:41.070
evaluated before batch disposition
under approved procedures.

40
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.750
Mix-up prevention is strongest when
multiple controls work together. These can

41
00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:50.710
include physical segregation,
material identification,

42
00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.190
approved artwork, line
clearance, in-process checks,

43
00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:59.110
barcode systems and independent review.
Each layer reduces a different failure

44
00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:04.170
opportunity. A single final inspection
is not an adequate substitute for

45
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.880
controlling risks throughout
packaging operations.

46
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.060
Imagine that an operator notices
a carton printed for a different

47
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.820
strength at the packaging line. The
operator should secure the operation

48
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.840
and report the finding under site procedures.
Authorized personnel should assess

49
00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.440
the extent of possible mix-up,
affected materials and batches, and any

50
00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:29.680
required investigation. Packaging should
resume only after the issue is appropriately

51
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.840
resolved and documented. The goal
is to protect patient safety and

52
00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.010
preserve traceability.
